work_order.approach:
0. WHERE THE APP'S DATA FILE ACTUALLY LIVES IN THE QA JOB — read this before writing a single verification recipe, because a bare relative path here reads correctly in a developer's checkout and silently means something else in CI.

  In compose mode the code under test is NOT the workspace root. .github/workflows/sdlc-qa.yml:248-260 checks it out with `path: _pr`, so the app lives at `${workspace}/_pr`. The boot step at :293-303 runs `nohup bash -c "cd _pr && $BOOT"` with env.boot = `npm run sdlc:serve`, so the server process has cwd `${workspace}/_pr`. src/store.js:4 computes `DEFAULT_PATH = path.resolve('data','groups.json')` from that cwd, so the file the server opens, reads and writes is **`_pr/data/groups.json`** — never `data/groups.json` at the workspace root. The workflow's own seed step (:347-353) agrees: it is the only step that carries `working-directory: _pr`, and it runs `npm run sdlc:seed` there.

  QA's shell, by contrast, runs at `${workspace}` — the 'Test it, adversarially' step (:391-418) sets no working-directory, and the only steps in the file that do are :263, :286 and :347. Nothing in .sdlc/agents/qa.md or .sdlc/memory/qa/environment.md tells QA this, which is why every recipe below spells the path out in full rather than using a relative one. Two traps follow, and both fail silently rather than loudly:

  (a) The workspace root is itself a checkout of this same repository, and its package.json carries the same `sdlc:seed` script. So a bare `npm run sdlc:seed` from QA's cwd SUCCEEDS — it writes `${workspace}/data/groups.json`, a file the server never opens. The corruption and the repair would land in different directories and the endpoint would keep answering 200. Every seed and restore in this plan is therefore `cd _pr && npm run sdlc:seed`, or equivalently `npm --prefix _pr run sdlc:seed` (both were checked to run with cwd inside the package). Every corruption and deletion is written to the literal path `_pr/data/groups.json`. The same trap applies to `data/` itself: the root's `data/` is gitignored and absent, so a `printf ... > data/groups.json` either fails outright or, after a `mkdir`, writes a file nothing reads. Use `mkdir -p _pr/data` before writing if in doubt; the workflow's seed step has already created it, so this is belt and braces.

  (b) The server re-reads the file on every request — load() has no cache — so there is no delay for a fix to take effect and no caching that could rescue a wrong path. The wrong path simply produces a correct-looking 200 forever.

  The rest of the plan is three changes, smallest first, all in existing modules.

1. src/store.js — the leak stops at the module boundary. Add an exported `StoreUnreadableError extends Error` with a fixed, safe message ('Group data store could not be read'), a `code` property of 'STORE_UNREADABLE', and the original error kept on `.cause` for the server's own stderr. Wrap the body of `load()` in try/catch — the `fs.readFileSync` and the `JSON.parse`, nothing else — and rethrow as `StoreUnreadableError`. `load()` still returns `{}` when the file does not exist: a first run serves an empty group list, so it is healthy and must stay healthy. Add an async `check()` to both stores: the file store attempts `fs.promises.readFile(p, 'utf8')` and `JSON.parse`s it, resolving on success and resolving ALSO on `err.code === 'ENOENT'` (an absent file is a first run, not a failure), and rethrowing anything else as `StoreUnreadableError`; `MemoryStore.check()` resolves. It uses `fs.promises`, not the sync path, so it never blocks the event loop and a timer can still fire while it is in flight. `createStore()` returns `check` alongside `load`/`save`/`reset` in both modes. Doing the ENOENT test inside the catch rather than an `existsSync` before the read is deliberate: an `existsSync`/read pair races a file that is created or deleted between the two, and in the delete direction it would report 503 for exactly the first-run case load() answers `{}` on.

2. src/server.js — /healthz asks, and the error is mapped in the two places it can surface. The handler becomes:

    // GET /healthz
    if (route.resource === 'healthz') {
      if (req.method !== 'GET') {
        return errorResponse(res, 'METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED', 'Method not allowed', 405);
      }
      try {
        await withDeadline(store.check());
      } catch {
        return errorResponse(res, 'STORE_UNREADABLE', 'Group data store could not be read', 503);
      }
      return jsonResponse(res, 200, { status: 'ok' });
    }

  `withDeadline(promise)` races against a timer and rejects with `StoreUnreadableError` if the timer wins; the timer is cleared in a `finally` so a pending timer never holds the event loop open and hangs `node --test`. The budget is `Number(process.env.HEALTH_TIMEOUT_MS) || 2000`, read INSIDE the handler on every request (not at module load) so a test can set it. A store object with no `check` throws a TypeError inside that try and is answered 503 — from a probe's point of view a store it cannot interrogate is one it cannot vouch for — and a test below asserts both factory branches do expose `check`, so that masking cannot hide a regression.

  The healthy response stays byte-identical to today's `{"status":"ok"}` — the ticket puts any change to it out of scope, and it is already distinguishable from the failure (200 + a `status` key vs 503 + an `error` key). The unhealthy response is the standard envelope, `{ error: { code: 'STORE_UNREADABLE', message: 'Group data store could not be read' } }`, at 503 — 503 rather than 500 because the app is fine and its dependency is not, and 503 is the status a probe or load balancer acts on. No field of the store file appears in the body. The handler never touches `err.message`, so nothing from the store reaches that body even when the failure is one we did not classify.

  Two mapping sites, because `store.load()` is called from seven places and six of them are outside any try/catch: the outer catch in `start()` (src/server.js:362-364), which the six reach, and the POST /api/groups catch (src/server.js:173-178), whose `store.load()` at :157 sits inside the try. Both add, before their existing branches, `if (err instanceof StoreUnreadableError) return storeUnreadableResponse(res);`. `storeUnreadableResponse` is one helper beside `errorResponse`. Every store-reading route then answers 503 `STORE_UNREADABLE` instead of 500 `INTERNAL`, and no response anywhere can carry a fragment of the data file.

3. Tests — the failure is covered everywhere the 200 is, and the leak is asserted with a planted marker string rather than by reading the message.

Screens/interfaces: this ticket has no UI. /healthz is JSON with no consumers in public/index.html (grepped: no reference), so there is no empty, loading, partial or error state to design, no focus to move, and no copy to invent beyond the two strings written out above. The response IS the surface, and its states are: 200 {status:ok}, 503 {error:{code,message}}, and 405 {error:{code:METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED}} for a non-GET.

review.detail:
check() accepts any file that JSON.parse accepts, but every consumer of load() requires a non-null object, so /healthz still answers 200 {'status':'ok'} on a store file whose every data route 500s — the precise failure the PR's own title names. The probe is now the only thing standing between the health check and the truth, and it tests a weaker condition than the routes do.

`src/store.js:55`

Evidence: Booted the real server (src/server.js start()) with STORE_PATH at a temp file, one file content per row, and hit /healthz and the data routes. Output:

### null file "null"
    200 GET /healthz :: {"status":"ok"}
    500 GET /api/groups :: {"error":{"code":"INTERNAL","message":"Cannot convert undefined or null to object"}}
    500 POST /api/groups :: {"error":{"code":"INTERNAL","message":"Cannot convert undefined or null to object"}}
### string file "\"hello\""
    200 GET /healthz :: {"status":"ok"}
    200 GET /api/groups :: {"groups":["h","e","l","l","o"]}
    500 POST /api/groups :: {"error":{"code":"INTERNAL","message":"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'trim')"}}
### number file "123"
    200 GET /healthz :: {"status":"ok"}
    200 GET /api/groups :: {"groups":[]}
    500 POST /api/groups :: {"error":{"code":"INTERNAL","message":"Cannot create property '78e4...' on number '123'"}}

The 500s are thrown by server.js:171 (Object.values(data) on null), server.js:199 (existing.name.trim() on a character) and server.js:204 (data[group.id] = group on a number) — all downstream of load(), which at store.js:38 returned the parsed value unchecked. check() at store.js:55 calls JSON.parse(raw) and discards the result, so the two disagree. Empty and truncated files ('{"a":1}trailing') do correctly 503, so this is the parseable-but-unusable gap only. No AC covers it: AC-2 is scoped to 'not valid JSON' and null is valid JSON, and work-order residual_risks only anticipated the BALANCES_INVARIANT case, which at least has its own typed code — this one surfaces as untyped 500 INTERNAL. Not data loss: every one of these throws before store.save() is reached (verified — the file was unchanged after the POSTs).

Fix: In check(), hold the parsed value and reject unless it is a non-null plain object, so the probe asserts the same contract load()'s consumers actually rely on:

  const parsed = JSON.parse(raw);
  if (parsed === null || typeof parsed !== 'object' || Array.isArray(parsed)) {
    throw new StoreUnreadableError(new TypeError('group store is not an object'));
  }

Add the matching case to test/store.test.js ('check() rejects with StoreUnreadableError when the file holds a JSON primitive or array') and one to the test/server.test.js:1374 loop asserting /healthz 503 alongside the 503 the data routes already return. Doing it in check() rather than at each Object.values() call site keeps the one-guard-in-the-shared-function shape the work order chose deliberately.

The four new store tests restore the env with `process.env.STORE_PATH = prev`, which in Node assigns the string "undefined" rather than unsetting the variable, so each one leaves STORE_PATH pointing at a file literally named `undefined` for every test that follows in the same process. The PR's own server-side helper gets this right — withFileStore (test/server.test.js:1270) uses `if (prev === undefined) delete process.env.STORE_PATH` — so the two halves of the same change use opposite conventions. Harmless in today's ordering (the two tests after these are memory-mode and do not touch the path), but the next file-mode case added to this file will silently read and write <repo>/undefined, and .gitignore covers only data/ and node_modules/.

`test/store.test.js:113`

Evidence: node -e "delete process.env.FOO; process.env.FOO = undefined; console.log(typeof process.env.FOO, JSON.stringify(process.env.FOO))" -> string "undefined". Reproducing the sequence the new tests follow (save a group, restore with `process.env.STORE_PATH = prev`, then createStore().save()):
  round-trip ok: {"g1":{"id":"g1",...}}
  after restore, STORE_PATH = "undefined"
  a file literally named "undefined" now exists in cwd: true
  load() now reads: {"g2":...}
— the second save landed in cwd and the original temp file was orphaned, though the read-back happened to match because the write and the read both used the same wrong path, so a round-trip assertion would still pass and hide it. Lines 113, 130, 145, 165 and 182 all use the unguarded form; line 32 and 84 predate this PR and share it.

Fix: Use the pattern the PR already introduced in withFileStore, in all five new finally blocks in test/store.test.js (and, if you touch them, the two pre-existing ones at :32 and :84):
  if (prev === undefined) delete process.env.STORE_PATH;
  else process.env.STORE_PATH = prev;

check() is a second, hand-written implementation of the store's read contract rather than a shared one, and the two have already drifted: load() returns whatever JSON.parse produced (store.js:38) while check() discards it and only asserts 'it parsed' (store.js:55). Every future tightening of what a usable store file looks like has to be made in two places or the health check and the routes will disagree again — which is exactly what happens today, and the same disagreement is reviewer A's finding 0 in its sharpest form. The async probe is the right shape (a synchronous read cannot be interrupted by a timer, so reusing load() would forfeit the deadline the ticket explicitly asks for), but the sync/async split is a reason to share the parse step, not to duplicate it.

`src/store.js:51`

Evidence: src/store.js:31-42 and 51-62 are the two reads, and only the second is new. Their only shared content today is the literal `JSON.parse` call. Booting the real server with STORE_PATH at a file containing `null`: /healthz answers 200 {"status":"ok"} while GET /api/groups answers 500 {"error":{"code":"INTERNAL","message":"Cannot convert undefined or null to object"}} thrown by Object.values(data) at src/server.js:171. Same for `"hello"`, `123` and `[1,2]`. So the two functions already return different answers about one file, and the drift is not hypothetical. Note also that applying reviewer A's proposed fix to check() alone inverts the disagreement rather than removing it: /healthz would then 503 while the data routes still 500 on the same file.

Fix: Extract the read's meaning into one function and have both entry points call it, so they cannot diverge:

  function parseGroups(raw) {
    const parsed = JSON.parse(raw);
    if (parsed === null || typeof parsed !== 'object' || Array.isArray(parsed)) {
      throw new TypeError('group store is not an object');
    }
    return parsed;
  }

load() becomes `return parseGroups(fs.readFileSync(p, 'utf8'))` inside its existing try, and check() becomes `parseGroups(await fs.promises.readFile(p, 'utf8'))` inside its. Both throws are already caught and rethrown as StoreUnreadableError, so the change is confined to src/store.js and nothing else moves. This closes reviewer A's finding 0 properly: every route reading a primitive store file answers 503 STORE_UNREADABLE instead of an untyped 500, and the health check and the data routes can no longer disagree. Add the primitive case to the existing loop in test/server.test.js that already walks several file contents, and to test/store.test.js.
